 the in  politics there's often been demonization  of opponents that's nothing new but the  sort of particular flavor of  demonization of opponents that we've  seen by Trump which is riddled with  these sort of egocentric delusions  that's another way in which sort of the  rhetoric of the Trump camp  struck me as similar to pre-world War  two Germany what do you think about that  I think that's true although I'm the  parallel to which I would coin is less  Hitler than Mussolini  mm-hmm and one often thinks in fact  Trump allows his chin to jut out in a  mussolini Escalade I don't know whether  it's done consciously  but in that respect the what you're  describing looks more a little more to  me like Italian fascism which for all  its evil was not quite as horrific as  German Nazism then it does like the like  German Nazism but there's no doubt that  it's there and once again it's it's  worth emphasizing without laying too  much weight on it that that sort of  thing was there in the in the early  1930s in the United States - there was a  lot of pageantry in the early New Deal  and while there was no Twitter there  were fireside chats and radio addresses  some of the saying now this is not by  any means to datura frankly Roseau there  was a great president with with a brush  of Nazism at all but to say that there  are populist forms of government that  have been appealing in the United States  for a very very long time and it can  easily be turned to the wrong uses well  that's sort of the difference between a  progressive populism versus what's  described at least in in waves of rancor  by Hillard and Keith who write about  this as a regressive populism in other  words a populist message that scapegoats  typically the other as bearing the  responsibility for the status quo which  of course is presented as being  absolutely terrible and the sort of  charismatic in a way authoritarian esque  politician presents his candidacy as the  fix right we will it with Hitler's case  it was we're gonna retrieve we're gonna  get back German pride we're going to get  revenge on those who have hurt the  country we are going to fix the economy  unemployment with Trump it's you know  build the wall get these 11 million  undocumented immigrants out a moratorium  on Muslim immigration the I alone can  bring you a panacea I think is an  interesting  I think you're making an awfully good  case there there's a lot to what you say  of course there's a question that's the  reverse of the question that I asked in  my book whether all of what we see in  the call it the the outright aspects of  Trump or in any way modelled on memories  of what went on in Europe in the middle  of the 20th century that I don't know  hmm  I can't speak that question but the  resemblances I think you're right are  there here's the last thing I want to  touch on from a legal perspective I have  many conversations with people who are  understandably worried about the  authoritarian tendencies at mostly so  far in rhetoric but also in terms of  legislative ideas from the current  administration here in the US and I know  many people who say whatever redder  rhetorical similarities we may find  between Trump and whoever else we want  to point to in history the legal  infrastructure of the United States when  it comes to the rights afforded by the  Bill of Rights when it comes to freedom  of the press etc that will prevent a  real move in the authoritarian direction  of the law that couldn't happen because  no matter what it is that the  administration ban and Trump whoever  would want to do our legal system would  not allow that to happen  is that reasonable to you or is that  something people should not immediately  write off in that way I don't know how  much our legal system would stand in the  way I think there is a culture of  democracy that's likely to stand in the  way mmm  that is you know things could have  turned quite bad in the 1930s and they  didn't I don't think in 